4. THE SQUARE FIGURE-8 TOROID — HYSTERESIS LAYER 1

One element. Two square lobes joined at a crossing.

```
    ┌─────────┐     ┌─────────┐
    │  LOBE 1 │─────│  LOBE 2 │
    └─────────┘  X  └─────────┘
                  ↑
              crossing point
```

Square lobes. Square B-H loop. One toroid.

Per-cell memory.

The toroid reads the leans. It does not drive them.

The three pairs lean. Their combined state appears as flux in the toroid. When the flux crosses Hc, the toroid flips.

The flip is the reference. The flip is the write.

One event. Two names. Same state.

· Old action — what was just done.
· New view — what's now being read.

The flip is the hinge. The old action becomes the new view. No gap. No separate update.

```
    Old view (previous reference)
         │
         ▼
    Lean across the three pairs
         │
         ▼
    Flux crosses Hc → toroid flips
         │
         ▼
    New view = old action
         │
         ▼
    Next lean reads against it
```

Every flip advances the cycle by one step.

Construction:

1. Two square-cross-section ferrite blocks (Mo-Permalloy or Metglas).
2. Cut a 1mm gap in each.
3. Join at the crossing point.
4. One continuous magnetic path: Lobe 1 → cross → Lobe 2 → cross back.

Windings:

· Sense: 10 turns, 36 AWG, around Lobe 1 only.
· Drive: 50 turns, 30 AWG, figure-8 wrap through both lobes.

The figure-8 wrap is what nucleates the vortex.

---

5. THE VORTEX — ONE QUBIT

The toroid's flip nucleates a magnetic vortex. One vortex per toroid.

One qubit.

The qubit's observables are quadratic in its amplitudes. That's what makes it the quadratic.

· Two complex amplitudes in
· Four real observables out
· Four extremal points on the Bloch sphere
· Four views up
· Four actions down
· Sixteen couplings

Four is not chosen. It falls out of the qubit's own structure.

